{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794989714,"nonce":"h9W00637ebo+J1Nzf9EGCm44kbI=","title":"Add opt-in CRDT-backed assets to the metadata-authored asset system. A CRDT asset has a stable logical document ID and typed local view, supports local reads and writes while offline, and synchronizes with a configured remote replica. Normal embedded assets, dynamic downloads, saves and preferences acquire no CRDT/network dependency. Metadata names such as @:crdtAsset are provisional until the API is implemented.","message":"Identity/model: distinguish tenant/catalog/document ID, replica/actor ID, schema version, causal document revision and immutable content IDs for snapshots or referenced blobs. Local edits change the document revision; content-addressed snapshots/blobs remain immutable. World/entity instance identity and coordinates remain separate from asset content and the mutable document. KyuGraph is a rebuildable relationship/query projection of merged state, not the CRDT merge engine or authoritative local edit log.\n\nLocal-first API: expose a typed local read/view, explicit mutation/transaction methods and change notifications; report local durability and remote acknowledgement separately. Validate local edits against the declared schema before committing. Persist actor identity, operation IDs/causal metadata, pending operations and a recoverable snapshot before acknowledging a durable local write. Reopen offline without a network service. Use application/tenant/document-scoped hxn-file storage on native/mobile and browser IndexedDB transactions. Current hxn-file atomic file replacement is not a multi-file transaction: design one atomic snapshot+journal record or an explicit recoverable journal protocol rather than assuming grouped file writes are atomic.\n\nMerge semantics must match data: text/sequence CRDTs for text and ordered collections, map/register semantics for keyed metadata, counters/sets where appropriate. Specify conflict behavior and whether concurrent register values are retained or a deterministic winner is selected. Do not byte-merge images/audio/arbitrary binary payloads; model their immutable content references and variants with an explicit concurrent-update policy. Validate mature candidates such as Automerge or Yjs/yrs for wire stability, schema/API fit, licensing, document growth and supported runtime/wasm/mobile integration before choosing an implementation. Keep the client contract independent of a particular relay provider.\n\nSync transport: WS/WSS for bidirectional operation/snapshot/ack streams, with HTTP(S) bootstrap/snapshot/blob transfer where useful. Protocol carries version, tenant/document identity, actor/operation IDs, causal heads/state vectors, snapshot/epoch and acknowledgement/resume state. Apply duplicate/reordered messages idempotently; request missing operations or a full snapshot after a gap. Preserve pending local edits across disconnects and process restarts. Bound message sizes, queued operations, outstanding bytes and retries; define cancellation, backoff and explicit sync/error state. Do not reconnect an explicitly closed/unloaded asset. No hidden network fetch during compilation.\n\nCloud/self-hosted operation: remote sync is application-configured and optional. Cloudflare Durable Objects can coordinate document sessions, durable relay state and acknowledgements; object storage holds immutable snapshots/blobs; cloud KyuGraph receives idempotent post-merge projection batches via a durable outbox. Do not assume one transaction spans local storage, DO, object storage and KyuGraph. Compatible open-source/self-hosted replicas use the same protocol. Authentication is application-supplied, scoped to tenant/document capabilities, with no service secret in a game or browser. Define logout/account changes, revocation, local namespace separation and recovery of unsynced edits.\n\nCompaction/GC: checkpoint snapshots without losing unacknowledged local operations; retain causal/tombstone information needed by offline peers. Define peer watermarks or epoch/resnapshot rules before garbage-collecting history. Keep blob references alive while documents, pending operations, snapshots or retention windows still need them. Support schema migration/version negotiation and recovery from interrupted journal writes or incompatible remote snapshots.\n\nAcceptance: two or more replicas can edit offline, reopen, reconnect and converge under concurrent edits, duplicate delivery, reordered/missing operations and interrupted sync; local writes are durable before success; queued edits survive app/browser restarts; conflict semantics are tested for each supported data type; binary references do not lose concurrent versions silently; cancellation/unload and tenant isolation work; quota/disk failures are recoverable; compacted replicas can resync long-offline peers. Run native HashLink/Ash/hxcpp, browser Ash wasm and both Emscripten modes, and real iOS/Android persistence/transitions. Verify the self-hosted and managed relay contracts with the same conformance suite.\n\nRelated: file-access 49624d318b0b2d8efec7e26ccd271c6004e5150e4fe8b5dd33e105ee21c472fb; metadata/content identity/KyuGraph projection af5e85a7d37b1f7b459064108b6797a39ae2b11f45eaa947e2cc8406174b0146; hosted service 3efcf240e5267cee2e7aae0cbc2b82cedd6a0a1bde3c7891b9f86817127071de. This issue plans CRDT assets; it does not claim an implemented CRDT backend.","files":null}]}